987
00:47:01.969 --> 00:47:04.449
the outside, it's to present the world in front

988
00:47:04.449 --> 00:47:06.810
of us in a different way so as to

989
00:47:06.810 --> 00:47:08.810
bring out this kind of critical perspective.

990
00:47:10.550 --> 00:47:15.389
So earlier I asked you what kinds of approaches

991
00:47:15.389 --> 00:47:22.820
to needs would lead to normative consequences. I mean,

992
00:47:22.830 --> 00:47:27.340
would generate normative forces, but do you think that

993
00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:34.149
approaching needs as neutral acts of pure description, can

994
00:47:34.149 --> 00:47:38.149
it serve the interests of oppressive regimes actually?

995
00:47:38.899 --> 00:47:40.669
Mhm. And I'm, I mean, and I think this

996
00:47:40.669 --> 00:47:43.500
idea of pure description, is a really interesting one.

997
00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:45.870
Like, what do we mean by a pure description?

998
00:47:45.969 --> 00:47:47.979
And I think Marx and Wittgenstein would both say

999
00:47:47.979 --> 00:47:50.500
this whole idea of a pure description is a

1000
00:47:50.699 --> 00:47:53.139
fantasy. It is a, is a social fantasy we

1001
00:47:53.139 --> 00:47:56.689
create within a particular social and historical order that

1002
00:47:56.689 --> 00:47:59.580
imagines we can just tell things purely descriptively. When

1003
00:47:59.580 --> 00:48:02.735
we describe, we do. So as socially embedded beings

1004
00:48:02.735 --> 00:48:05.014
with a particular sort of consciousness. We describe four

1005
00:48:05.014 --> 00:48:09.455
reasons and in ways that invoke a particular social

1006
00:48:09.455 --> 00:48:12.554
order that isn't neutral, right, that isn't pure itself,

1007
00:48:13.175 --> 00:48:15.294
that favors some interests over others, has struggle, and

1008
00:48:15.294 --> 00:48:17.695
so on. So, once we kind of explode this

1009
00:48:17.695 --> 00:48:20.614
idea of pure description as a, as a kind

1010
00:48:20.614 --> 00:48:23.854
of philosophical possibility, what happens when we do imagine

1011
00:48:23.854 --> 00:48:25.995
that we could do pure description, we present things

1012
00:48:25.995 --> 00:48:29.270
as pure description. And I think Marx's way of

1013
00:48:29.270 --> 00:48:30.989
telling that story is something like, well, look, all

1014
00:48:30.989 --> 00:48:34.030
this does is just disappear the politics that's there,

1015
00:48:34.110 --> 00:48:38.070
the struggle, the harms, the power. Pure description just

1016
00:48:38.070 --> 00:48:40.709
masks all that stuff that's going on to present

1017
00:48:40.709 --> 00:48:45.629
itself as pure and neutral. Um, SO that neutralizes

1018
00:48:45.629 --> 00:48:48.949
politics. It removes possibilities of struggle, it makes things

1019
00:48:48.949 --> 00:48:51.840
seem necessary when in fact they are contestable. And

1020
00:48:51.840 --> 00:48:54.889
in that sense they can serve oppressive regimes because

1021
00:48:54.889 --> 00:48:57.330
if you can successfully present something as a pure

1022
00:48:57.330 --> 00:49:00.530
description, then you can take away possibilities for conflict,

1023
00:49:00.610 --> 00:49:02.989
dissent, you can, you can make power look natural,

1024
00:49:03.219 --> 00:49:03.830
things like that.

1025
00:49:04.969 --> 00:49:08.550
Yeah, so, uh, I have one final question then.

1026
00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:11.969
What would you say are the main implications of

1027
00:49:11.969 --> 00:49:17.729
this Marxian approach to needs for the contemporary politics

1028
00:49:17.729 --> 00:49:21.810
of need? I mean, looking at how the politics

1029
00:49:21.810 --> 00:49:27.649
of need presents itself contemporarily, what would be the

1030
00:49:27.649 --> 00:49:30.510
implications of this more Marxian approach?

1031
00:49:30.929 --> 00:49:34.129
Um, AND I think there's, there's two main ones

1032
00:49:34.129 --> 00:49:37.489
I'll draw out here. Um, YOU know, one of

1033
00:49:37.489 --> 00:49:39.409
them, I think it, it, it is conceptual, but

1034
00:49:39.409 --> 00:49:41.610
it's a conceptual move that opens up the politics,

1035
00:49:41.689 --> 00:49:43.850
and that is, you know, in so many of

1036
00:49:43.850 --> 00:49:46.550
contemporary needs theory and it, and it needs talk

1037
00:49:46.550 --> 00:49:48.489
more generally in, you know, you know, political spheres

1038
00:49:48.489 --> 00:49:51.489
in practice. The question's always driven towards, you know,

1039
00:49:51.610 --> 00:49:53.989
what. What do people really need? What are the

1040
00:49:53.989 --> 00:49:55.870
facts of needs, and what do we do about

1041
00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:59.760
those facts? And what I think Marx pushes us

1042
00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:01.629
to do is to ask different sorts of questions

1043
00:50:01.629 --> 00:50:05.209
like what had to happen for needs to appear

1044
00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:07.810
in the way that they do to us today.

1045
00:50:08.229 --> 00:50:11.750
And when things happen that way, what politically led

1046
00:50:11.750 --> 00:50:13.540
to that happening and, and, and what were the

1047
00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:18.310
consequences for us? Who wins, who loses, who gets

1048
00:50:18.310 --> 00:50:22.169
harmed, who gets power, things like that. Whose interests

1049
00:50:22.169 --> 00:50:24.570
are served. And then, you know, that opens up

1050
00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:26.120
questions of how things can do differently. So to

1051
00:50:26.120 --> 00:50:30.189
sort of switch around from what needs fundamentally are

1052
00:50:30.729 --> 00:50:32.679
to what does our talk about needs and our

1053
00:50:32.679 --> 00:50:34.399
way of thinking about needs do in the world,

1054
00:50:34.409 --> 00:50:36.169
and what, you know, should we accept it as

1055
00:50:36.169 --> 00:50:38.040
it is? Should we try to change it. I

1056
00:50:38.040 --> 00:50:39.969
think that's one element. A second element is probably

1057
00:50:39.969 --> 00:50:41.770
the centrality of work, and I spoke about that

1058
00:50:41.770 --> 00:50:43.810
very briefly with this idea of the wage contract.

1059
00:50:43.850 --> 00:50:45.600
And I, and I think there are, there are

1060
00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:47.610
others who have sort of emphasized, uh, elements of

1061
00:50:47.610 --> 00:50:50.540
the politics of need in different ways. And what

1062
00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:52.699
I think Marx's analysis does is, is to reveal

1063
00:50:52.699 --> 00:50:54.679
the, you know, key to getting a, a really

1064
00:50:54.679 --> 00:50:57.419
good critical perspective on the politics of needs is

1065
00:50:57.419 --> 00:51:00.419
to understand the kind of underlying structures of power

1066
00:51:00.419 --> 00:51:02.780
within that politics, you know, how do those structures

1067
00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:05.860
come about, who wins, who loses. Um, AND, and

1068
00:51:05.860 --> 00:51:08.320
his part of that story really centers on work,

1069
00:51:08.620 --> 00:51:12.169
the role of needs in, um, this, this, in

1070
00:51:12.169 --> 00:51:15.459
rendering us dependent on work to live, and how

1071
00:51:15.459 --> 00:51:18.760
that not only. HAS an important role in, in,

1072
00:51:18.830 --> 00:51:23.050
in kind of uh. Uh, CREATING a kind of

1073
00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:25.729
dependence on work, um, but how that also shapes

1074
00:51:25.729 --> 00:51:27.010
our needs. And, you know, we can see that

1075
00:51:27.010 --> 00:51:29.000
in the world around us, how often our needs

1076
00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:33.149
are shaped by the needs of work, our education,

1077
00:51:33.250 --> 00:51:34.889
our healthcare, all these sorts of things are often

1078
00:51:34.889 --> 00:51:37.929
really strongly centered around, uh, the role of work

1079
00:51:37.929 --> 00:51:40.010
in shaping our needs. And what I think Marx

1080
00:51:40.010 --> 00:51:42.330
does is bring that work story back into the

1081
00:51:42.330 --> 00:51:46.010
critical conversation, re-centers it, and that I think provides

1082
00:51:46.010 --> 00:51:48.040
a really useful kind of critical perspective on the

1083
00:51:48.040 --> 00:51:49.290
contemporary politics of need.

1084
00:51:49.989 --> 00:51:53.429
Mhm. But, but I mean the way we understand

1085
00:51:53.429 --> 00:51:57.939
work and the implications that ask for needs in

1086
00:51:57.939 --> 00:52:01.629
our contemporary society, I, I mean that, that, that's

1087
00:52:01.629 --> 00:52:07.310
again, uh, how work. Um, WORKS, I mean, so

1088
00:52:07.310 --> 00:52:10.010
to speak, uh, um, I mean, uh, how work

1089
00:52:10.419 --> 00:52:14.949
is done in this, uh, capitalist system, right? I

1090
00:52:14.949 --> 00:52:19.949
mean, because in other kinds of social organizations, uh,

1091
00:52:20.030 --> 00:52:22.149
I, I don't know if we lived in a

1092
00:52:22.149 --> 00:52:26.739
hunter-gatherer society or a hor a horticulturalist society or

1093
00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:33.750
Um, an agricultural feudalist society, the way we would

1094
00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:37.590
understand work and the need we would attach to

1095
00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:42.790
it would again be, uh, contextual. I mean, and

1096
00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:45.429
it would be different from how we understand it

1097
00:52:45.429 --> 00:52:49.070
in this capitalist context, correct?

1098
00:52:49.389 --> 00:52:50.870
Yeah, that's, I mean, and, and much of what

1099
00:52:50.870 --> 00:52:53.149
we've said today about, about need could probably be

1100
00:52:53.149 --> 00:52:55.030
said about work as well. Work itself is a

1101
00:52:55.030 --> 00:52:58.330
particular historical form that. Takes a particular kind of

1102
00:52:58.330 --> 00:53:00.669
kind of shape in in what we might call

1103
00:53:00.669 --> 00:53:04.830
the work society, um, and our understanding of what

1104
00:53:04.830 --> 00:53:06.620
work is and its role in our life is,

1105
00:53:06.629 --> 00:53:08.310
is, is shaped by that society and needs is

1106
00:53:08.310 --> 00:53:10.449
a really big part of that story. Um, AND

1107
00:53:10.449 --> 00:53:12.260
I think what the, and it brings into conversation,

1108
00:53:12.370 --> 00:53:14.489
you know, there is a, an active conversation in,

1109
00:53:14.550 --> 00:53:17.050
in political practice and in political theory about the

1110
00:53:17.050 --> 00:53:19.760
work society, what it does, and, you know, looking

1111
00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:22.989
at sort of post-work theories, which are increasingly prominent.

1112
00:53:23.409 --> 00:53:25.969
And I think what the, the Marxian approach to

1113
00:53:25.969 --> 00:53:28.290
me does very effectively is to bring these two

1114
00:53:28.719 --> 00:53:31.989
kind of discourses into, into useful conversation and say,

1115
00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:33.570
you know, one of the really important ways we

1116
00:53:33.570 --> 00:53:35.649
have to understand the work society is to understand

1117
00:53:35.649 --> 00:53:37.649
how it's created a particular sort of human life

1118
00:53:37.649 --> 00:53:40.590
with a particular set of needs. Those needs feel

1119
00:53:40.590 --> 00:53:42.439
very natural and appear very natural, and in fact

1120
00:53:42.439 --> 00:53:45.350
they, you know, they are important right now. It's

1121
00:53:45.350 --> 00:53:46.949
not to say that they don't matter, they do

1122
00:53:46.949 --> 00:53:49.669
matter, but precisely because we are historical beings who

1123
00:53:49.669 --> 00:53:51.189
live in the world that we do, these things

1124
00:53:51.189 --> 00:53:53.540
are really important. We can't just pretend they aren't

1125
00:53:53.540 --> 00:53:56.590
there. But they could be different, and then we

1126
00:53:56.590 --> 00:53:59.530
have to open these critical questions about, well, look,

1127
00:54:00.239 --> 00:54:02.270
what kind of society might generate a different sort

1128
00:54:02.270 --> 00:54:04.129
of human life and a different set of needs?

1129
00:54:04.429 --> 00:54:07.110
In what ways might that release us from relationships

